SM(445,{"n":"Introduction to Acts","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Introduction to Acts.mp3","d":2836.0,"s":[[0.56,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, now an Orthodox priest serving at St."],[16.8,"George Orthodox Cathedral in Charleston, West Virginia."],[21.68,"And tonight we're starting the book of Acts, which is really an abbreviated title."],[30.08,"The actual title of the book is The Acts of the Apostles."],[33.52,"And sometimes you'll even see it extended beyond that with some more adjectives, like the Acts of the Holy Apostles or the Acts of the Holy and All Audible Apostles, etc. etc."],[46.8,"But uh Acts of the Apostles is really the title of the book, and that gets abbreviated just to the book of Acts."],[57.12,"And this book, the Act of the Apostles, Acts of the Apostles is a little bit unique in the New Testament."],[66.76,"Most of the books of the New Testament, we've got the four Gospels, and then we've got St."],[73.56,"Paul's Epistles and the other epistles, St."],[76.52,"John's Epistles, St."],[78.2,"James's Epistle, St."],[79.8,"Peter's Epistles. So those are letters, and then we've got Gospels, and then we've also got the book of Acts and the Book of Revelation."],[88.76,"The book of Revelation has letters attached to it, so it's even a little closer."],[92.44,"But the book of Acts is a story, it's a narrative about the beginnings of the church, about uh the apostles."],[102.32,"It's going to pick up where the book of Luke, where the gospel according to St."],[107.68,"Luke left off, uh, which was with uh Christ's ascension into heaven."],[112.56,"We're gonna pick up again with Christ's ascension into heaven."],[115.44,"The story's basically going to be repeated as we begin this second part."],[121.28,"Uh, but this is, of course, the only narrative story type of book we have in the New Testament, other than the Gospels."],[130.48,"And the four Gospels, of course, center upon Jesus Himself, whereas the book of Acts uh centers upon the Apostles."],[139.84,"Uh, and sometimes we don't appreciate how unique that is because, of course, we've got the whole Bible together in one book between two covers, and there are plenty of books. in the Old Testament"],[151.16,"There are sort of historical narrative telling stories about people and people who aren't Jesus."],[157.64,"You know. And so when we see Acts, we think of it sort of in those terms."],[164.2,"But what's important about the sort of uniqueness of Acts is that when we read those books in the Old Testament that are telling stories about Moses or about Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob in the book of Genesis, or about David, or about the prophet Elijah or the Prophet Elisha, as we read through those books, you know, and we come to understand them, we see how they're interpreted in the New Testament."],[190.12,"Often they're interpreted as sort of pointing to Christ or pointing to events in the life of Christ."],[194.84,"John the Forerunner, St"],[195.84,"We see these connections between the prophet Elijah and St."],[201.68,"John the Baptist."],[203.28,"We see these connections between Jesus as the Messiah and King David."],[209.68,"You know, and so we sort of read these in this sort of prophetic way."],[213.04,"Well, the book of Acts takes place after Christ."],[217.68,"And so you can't sort of read it in a prophetic way because it comes after the fact."],[223.36,"It comes after the fact."],[225.28,"And so this argues against, and uh in the Orthodox Church, we may take this for granted, but it's not taken uh for granted among our Protestant friends, uh, that part of what this communicates to us is that the lives of the saints, the lives of the people who have followed Christ historically, are significant."],[250.68,"Are significant. There is a certain element within Protestantism that says, you know, well, we just need Jesus."],[260.06,"Why would you ask a saint to pray for you?"],[261.98,"Uh just pray to Jesus."],[263.6,"Jesus is what's really important."],[264.88,"And of course, Jesus being God, there's a certain truth to that."],[269.6,"But at the same time, the scriptures show us."],[272.85,"Not just in the Old Testament, where things can be interpreted as prophetic of Jesus, but here in the book of the book of Acts, that these other people, St."],[282.45,"Peter, St. Paul, St."],[284.01,"James, their lives, what they did, their acts, the things they did, the things they taught, are also significant."],[291.93,"They're also significant."],[293.37,"And this runs against our uh tendency, even in in uh the famous red letter Bibles."],[301.79,"Where they have all the words of Jesus in red letters."],[304.03,"Like those are the parts that are really, really Bible."],[310.03,"But of course, that isn't the case."],[312.13,"That isn't the case."],[313.65,"And so from the book of Acts, using it as an example, we find from a very early period in the church, as far as we could tell, from near the very beginning, we find uh the church recording the stories of holy people, of saints who lived among them."],[335.85,"And so there becomes this literature of the lives of the saints."],[339.53,"A lot of the early books in the second and third centuries are even called Acts."],[344.33,"So we have an Acts of Peter, there's an Acts, Acts of Paul and Thecla, telling us about the life of Saint Thecla."],[351.05,"Uh this literature kind of burgeons."],[354.17,"And of course, now in the Orthodox Church, it's again it's taken for granted."],[358.25,"We read, you know, the lives of the saints, we draw inspiration from them, we see, you know, uh different ways that people have been like Christ."],[368.33,"You know, we're all called to be like Christ by the scriptures, but all of us are. different"],[374.29,"You know, there are there are men and there are women, and there we have different times in our lives when we're young and when we're old, and when we're and so seeing the holy people of the past, the people of the past who have experienced salvation, seeing what they went through, we can find there, you know, people at whatever stage in life we are, whoever we are, wherever we are, whatever it is we're facing."],[396.37,"There are people who with Christ and in Christ have faced those things before, have dealt with those things before, have lived the kind of life we lived before."],[405.09,"And so that's not just an accidental thing, that's not something that develops later in the church that's taught to us right here by the very fact that the book of Acts, you know, is in the scriptures, is in the New Testament."],[419.29,"As we mentioned before we started, the book of the Acts of the Apostles is written by Saint Luke."],[426.25,"It's not just a second book written by Saint Luke after his gospel, but it is the second part of a two-part work."],[436.73,"It is a two-volume work."],[438.73,"The gospel according to St."],[440.01,"Luke in the book of Acts."],[442.09,"They're planned, they're put together."],[444.41,"And we're going to see themes that were developed in Saint Luke's Gospel are going to be carried forward here in the book of Acts."],[453.61,"Sort of seeds that were planted there are now going to come to fruition."],[458.81,"We're going to see connections between the life of Christ and the lives of his apostles."],[472.21,"Now there's some debate about this because Theophilus means the man who loves God."],[477.73,"So there are some people who take the the position that Theophilus was not an actual person."],[483.67,"And that St."],[484.47,"Luke is just writing to the man who loves God, meaning to people in general, who love God."],[492.49,"As opposed to to an individual named named Theophilus."],[496.57,"And the basis for that argument is really that the gospel according to St"],[501.25,"Luke and the Book of Acts really do have this sort of broad scope, where uh Saint Luke is trying to set this out."],[509.49,"It doesn't seem like something written for a particular person."],[514.06,"That this was intended to have a very wide audience."],[517.5,"But the answer to that is that Theophilus was most likely the patron who sort of sponsored the writing of the book of Luke, the book of Acts."],[530.94,"We live in a day of self publishing on the internet."],[535.18,"And so anybody who wants to can pretty much type something up and you know post it somewhere and probably even get it, you know, print to order, you know, self-published somewhere and have a book."],[549.22,"Um this, of course, was was. uh a millennium and a half before the printing press"],[556.66,"So when a book was written, just the materials, the vellum, the ink and everything, were fairly expensive to write it in the first place."],[565.86,"And then if you wanted anybody else to ever see it, you're gonna need to have copies made."],[570.5,"And to have those copies made, again, there's no photocopier, you had to have people sit there and by hand copy the whole thing out."],[578.9,"And if you wanted more than one copy, they had to do that multiple times, or you had to have multiple people all doing it at once."],[585.54,"That was an expensive proposition."],[588.24,"Because the on top of everything else, the literacy rate was very low at this point, and we're not even talking about the literacy rate in, say, Aramaic."],[595.44,"talking. We're about the literacy rate in Greek"],[603.0,"So we we sometimes take for granted uh how many people can read today."],[610.6,"Um at the time of World War One, yeah, so this is this is almost 1900 years after when St."],[620.12,"Luke is writing his two volumes."],[622.36,"Uh in at the beginning of World War I, the standard for literacy, what made you literate, according to the US government standard for the military and everything, was that you could sign your name."],[637.6,"Rather than just putting your mark."],[639.82,"You could actually sign your name."],[641.54,"That meant you were literate."],[643.22,"And the literacy rate in the US military in World War I was about 40% by that standard."],[651.46,"By that standard."],[652.74,"About 40% of people could sign their name."],[656.74,"So they weren't reading books."],[663.3,"And so it's a very recent thing, since the advent of public education and these kind of things, where just the average person can read a language."],[677.02,"So certainly at this time in history, the average person could not had no education, and the average person was not literate."],[689.34,"It was more common for people at that time in that world, in the Roman world, to be able to speak more than one language than it is in America, at least."],[697.18,"I know not all of us are from America."],[699.26,"In other countries, still, it's very common for people to know more than one language."],[706.3,"In America, most people only know one language now."],[713.42,"But at this time, it was very common for people to be able to speak and understand more than one language."],[718.78,"But reading and writing was not something that almost anyone could do."],[723.26,"So all this is to say to get a copy, to get a copy of one of of the books of the New Testament in the first century, they estimate would have cost the equivalent about $5,000 in today's money."],[742.58,"So if we're talking about St."],[745.7,"Luke's two-volume work, you're probably talking about $10,000 per copy."],[752.58,"So if this is going to be distributed and have any kind of wide audience, you're going to need a patron to sponsor it."],[761.44,"To devote the money to get that out there."],[763.84,"And so most likely that is who Theophilus was."],[767.68,"That it isn't that St."],[768.88,"Luke wrote this just for one person to read, but rather that he addresses it to The Theopophhilusil.us because is the one who's paying to get this book distributed"],[780.6,"So it's sort of dedicated to Theophilus rather than rather than addressed addressed to him per se."],[789.8,"Now St. Luke, this is another thing that it's hard hard for us to tell when we're reading it today, because when Bibles are translated, you know, they're usually translated by a committee, and the whole committee works on all the books, so they all come out sort of sounding the same."],[806.52,"You know, that's the English or the Arabic or whatever all sounds sort of the same from book to book to book."],[814.6,"In the original, in the Greek, the books of the New Testament have a very have very different writing styles."],[819.88,"Vastly different You can very much tell the level of education of the different authors, you know, when you read the when you read the different books."],[830.88,"And Saint Luke, in particular, in both uh his gospel and in the book of the Acts of the Apostles, is very deliberately using a certain Greek style."],[843.04,"And it's a Greek style that he adapted from the Greek historians, especially Thucydides, but also Herodotus and some of the other Greek historians."],[853.36,"So he is deliberately writing in a way to convey to the people who are reading it that this is a historical account."],[862.08,"That he is setting out sort of the history of of. the life of Jesus and then of the beginnings the church"],[870.76,"So there are some other books, you know."],[872.84,"The book of Hebrews, for example, looks more like a sermon than a letter."],[876.86,"They're sort of different, different genres, you know."],[880.54,"But he is very much writing as a historian."],[883.18,"And so the original people who read it, the original Greek, if they're educated enough to be able to read the Greek, would have also been familiar with these Greek histories, and they would have said, okay, so this is he's laying out history."],[897.23,"History here. And that's that's very important in our modern world, because, of course, there are a lot of people nowadays who want to try to turn the New Testament into mythology."],[908.43,"Say that Jesus is some kind of mythological character, this and that"],[913.27,"Well, there are plenty of mythological works in Greek."],[918.23,"Starting with Hesiod and Homer in 800 BC."],[922.39,"If that's what St."],[923.75,"Luke was setting out to do, he had a style he could have used to do that."],[930.71,"And he doesn't."],[931.99,"He's very deliberately chosen this historical style."],[936.39,"So he at least is intending to communicate this to us as these are things that actually happened."],[942.79,"These are the things that took place."],[944.47,"At the beginning of his gospel, he took the time to mention the fact that many people had had written accounts of parts of Jesus' life, and that he was sort of compiling them."],[953.91,"of He makes mentions the fact that he had spoken to eyewitnesses about all these things."],[959.63,"So he very much presented himself as a historian who did his research."],[965.39,"And the same is true, gonna be true for the first part of the Acts of the Apostles."],[972.59,"An interesting thing is gonna happen partway through."],[976.83,"It's a little before the halfway point."],[979.15,"We're gonna see that all of a sudden it's gonna go from speaking in the third person, meaning he did this, they did that, to speaking in the first person."],[989.71,"We did this, we did that."],[993.95,"And uh that's going to coincide with the time when Saint Luke joined Saint Paul and his other companions on his journeys."],[1006.95,"Uh, so there is going to be a portion, while he he did not claim to be an eyewitness to anything in the gospel, according to Saint Luke."],[1015.27,"So it's pretty clear he was not, he himself was not there in person to witness those things."],[1019.99,"He says he spoke to witnesses."],[1022.47,"A good portion of the book of the Acts of the Apostles is gonna be sort of his direct testimony to things that happened while he was there."],[1031.96,"As sort of his own his own eyewitness."],[1035.24,"And we're gonna see later on when we get into St."],[1038.2,"Paul's epistles, into his letters, he's gonna mention Luke as someone who was there with him him. who was a companion of his and who helped"],[1046.8,"And it's from there that we learn that Saint Luke was a physician."],[1054.64,"That he was the equivalent of a doctor of the first century."],[1058.0,"Now it has to be said, medicine of the first century AD was very different than medicine today."],[1066.72,"But it was still based in the study of the human body and human bodily system."],[1075.52,"It's progressed a lot now, not just technologically, but in terms of knowledge."],[1081.6,"But it's not a totally different thing now."],[1084.56,"You shouldn't think that St."],[1085.92,"Luke was like a witch doctor or something."],[1088.42,"That he was, you know, trying to do magic spells and or something to heal people."],[1093.86,"That's not the case."],[1095.22,"He was a physician in the same sense that doctors today are a physician and trying to study the human body and cure disease."],[1102.66,"It's just at that point in our history there was a lot less knowledge and a lot less technology."],[1110.1,"But that said, this means he was in the club most educated class of that day of the first century."],[1119.54,"This is why he was able to utilize, for example, that historical style, because he was fluent in Greek and had read the Greek histories and had read the literature."],[1130.18,"It is generally argued, and there's a good case for it, that Saint Luke was from Antioch."],[1138.06,"And there was, from the time of Antioch, the city of Antioch's founding, there was a very large Jewish population there."],[1148.38,"About a third of the population at the time of the founding was Jewish."],[1154.38,"And so there was a huge Jewish community there all the way into the 5th century AD."],[1163.82,"So there's some debate as to whether St."],[1166.86,"Luke himself was Jewish or whether he was a Greek or somewhere in between."],[1172.38,"Because of course there were Assyrian people there and people living in what's now Lebanon who were not Jewish but were not ethnically Greek either."],[1177.74,"who They were Semitic people were not Jewish."],[1186.42,"So it's possible because there were also a lot of those folks living in Antioch."],[1191.46,"So it's possible he fell into that category."],[1197.3,"But that's that's debated."],[1199.22,"We only have clues."],[1200.66,"It's nowhere said exactly where Saint Luke fit into that."],[1208.9,"And so the the book of the Acts of the Apostles is going to, as I mentioned, pick up right where the gospel according to Saint Luke left off with the ascension of Christ."],[1219.14,"It's that way to talk about the early Christian community, and then there's going to be a transition. where it's really going to start following Saint Paul after St"],[1229.18,"Paul's experience on the road to Damascus and then his missionary journeys."],[1232.94,"And then when we get to the end, we'll discuss this more."],[1236.22,"But at a certain point, the book of the Acts of the Apostles just sort of leaves off."],[1241.98,"It doesn't come, it doesn't end with the death of St."],[1244.54,"Paul and ends before the death of St."],[1246.46,"Paul. Some people have argued that, well, that must be when it was written, and that's why he left off there."],[1253.34,"There are other good reasons to believe that that's probably not the case."],[1257.98,"That it was written later, and he chose that for other for other reasons."],[1261.42,"But well, as I said, we'll talk about that more when we get to the end."],[1265.58,"But the book of the Acts of the Apostles is very much. open-ended in that way"],[1272.02,"The Gospel has a very clear, you know, it begins with the birth of St."],[1279.14,"John the Forerunner and then the birth of Christ, and then it ends with Christ's ascension into heaven."],[1283.46,"That's a pretty logical, you know, sort of unit."],[1287.62,"But the book of the Acts of the Apostles, it's not really a life of St."],[1290.9,"Paul, because the first dozen chapters really focus on a whole other group of people."],[1297.94,"And then most of those people sort of drop out for this for the second half to focus on St."],[1303.46,"Paul. So it's a very inter it's a very interestingly structured book."],[1311.42,"And finally, the last thing is. there are actually two different versions of the Book of Acts"],[1317.26,"There's a longer version and a shorter version."],[1321.34,"And what's really interesting about the longer version and the shorter version of the Book of Acts is that the longer version of the Book of Acts does not have it's about a third longer."],[1334.46,"But the longer version of the Book of Acts does not have any stories in it that aren't in the shorter version of the Book of Acts."],[1341.58,"All that's added is description, sort of details."],[1347.98,"So and that kind of thing."],[1349.34,"So the stories are kind of fleshed out these ways in more descriptive terms, more dramatic terms."],[1355.18,"There's gonna be a shipwreck, you know, later on, and that shipwreck is very very. dramatic in the longer version, you know, and a little more bare bones of the shorter version"],[1363.38,"Uh and so this too is the subject of a lot of debate people as to and uh one of the interesting things you you might think, well, okay, maybe the shorter version came first and people added to it, or maybe the longer version came first and they condensed it because of the cost of copying it or something."],[1382.66,"You know, they sort of boiled it down took out all the adjectives."],[1386.26,"Um it depends."],[1394.42,"The church has a history used both."],[1398.42,"Used both. In different times at different places."],[1401.06,"ces. That's in that's in our Archdioe Epistle Book"],[1408.06,"The English Archdiocese Epistle Book, I believe, uses the shorter version."],[1413.34,"The church, somebody said down who that was only published about five years ago."],[1418.86,"So and the Slavonic version, for example, that they use in Russia uses the longer versions."],[1424.78,"So we've used both."],[1427.18,"Like I said, they don't contradict each other."],[1429.5,"They don't, it's just a matter of description."],[1432.38,"And and our we have the oldest copies we have of the book of Acts come from both kinds."],[1444.06,"Going all the way back to the the beginning of the second century, the earliest copies we have."],[1448.14,"We have copies of the longer version and copies of the shorter version"],[1454.02,"So one theory, and this is the one that I I agree with is that be because of the fact that both seem to go all the way back, and that you find both of them in different parts of the church all the time, is that both of them came from Saint Luke."],[1478.18,"That they represent essentially two different graphs."],[1481.46,"That that he wrote one and then he edited it"],[1486.7,"And so as it was distributed and copies were made, there were copies of both."],[1492.54,"And so different churches just copied the ones they had."],[1496.88,"And so that's why you get this sort of mosaic of you who's using who's using which version."],[1503.76,"Um those who want to pay more could have worked the longer version."],[1508.14,"And and you you know that we take for granted all the communications we have."],[1513.26,"Yeah, that's true."],[1514.15,"So if in if in Rome they've got the longer version, and in Alexandria, they've got the shorter version, and in Antioch, they've got the longer version, in Constantinople, they've got the shorter version."],[1526.43,"They wouldn't know."],[1529.11,"No. It's not like they could go and let's compare our copies."],[1533.11,"Hey, you know, yours is a little longer right there."],[1534.95,"Yours has a couple extra words, you know, that didn't happen."],[1535.99,"So everybody just copied the version that they had"],[1542.59,"You know, and used used the version that they had."],[1546.11,"Um but it's an interesting thing, and and uh we'll we'll point to some places along the way because for the most part uh the Orthodox Study Bible has a lot of material from the longer version in it."],[1560.27,"It's not just using the longer version."],[1563.31,"Um the Orthodox Study Bible used the New King James, and the New King James Version, the the committee that did it kind of assessed at different points."],[1576.51,"And so sometimes they included things from the longer version, and sometimes they didn't."],[1580.99,"Um we'll try and we'll try and note those where they're important"],[1586.23,"I mean, some of them, if it's just one word, you know, I'm not gonna ruin your good time by pointing out every single individual word, but there are places where it's significant."],[1599.43,"You know how uh the apostles, most of them, if I'm correct, dictated their uh what they wanted to say to someone else."],[1608.47,"Yes. Do you think Saint Luke uh wrote it out himself, or do you think it was dictating it?"],[1615.19,"Um it's hard to tell."],[1616.55,"He doesn't say."],[1618.27,"Often when the the like in the epistles, um one of my favorite trick questions is I ask people who wrote the book of Romans."],[1625.83,"the book of And if you go to the end of Romans, it says I Tertius."],[1627.79,"And when they say St."],[1628.91,"Paul, I say no, it was Tertius."],[1633.29,"Because that was the name of the scribe who was writing it down."],[1636.65,"For Saint Paul."],[1637.61,"But usually that's indicated."],[1640.01,"There's some indication, usually at the very end, um, of who it is who did the actual scribing."],[1652.47,"Or St. Paul at a couple points too will comment, you know, I'm writing this with my own hand."],[1658.95,"You know. Um, so that that was not uncommon."],[1662.79,"St. Luke never says he's doing that."],[1665.03,"Now, certainly, if he has a sponsor and everything, it's very possible he's doing that."],[1669.67,"I mean, a lot of times that was, I mean, St."],[1670.71,"Paul was perfectly literate too"],[1674.91,"So that wasn't just done for the sake of, you know, I'm not a very good writer."],[1681.15,"You know, I need you to write this for him."],[1682.91,"It was also often done just because uh the written word and the spoken word are not identical."],[1692.59,"And so having somebody who's sort of it's like dictating a letter to a secretary who's a really good typist and can really sort of, you know, polish it up and make correct the grammar and you know, make sure everything is spot on, you know."],[1707.71,"Um and so the usual procedure for that would be the person would dictate it, it would be written down, then the person would read it it and and make whatever corrections you know that they wanted to make, and then the scribe would sort of finish, then it would be um then it would be delivered."],[1726.87,"And uh the person who delivered it also was a significant person, uh, because it wasn't they weren't there was no postal service."],[1734.85,"And so the person who was sent with the letter to whoever was getting it didn't just sort of walk up and you know toss it in their box and walk away."],[1744.05,"They would come and they would read it aloud to them, and that person would be expected to be prepared to answer questions."],[1752.69,"You know, if the person did if something wasn't clear, you know, what did he what what did he mean by whatever?"],[1757.17,"That person who delivered it would be expected to be able to explain."],[1759.65,"Oh, he was talking. about XYZ"],[1763.93,"So there was also another significant person there in the in the process."],[1768.81,"And so it's likely that St."],[1770.41,"Luke would have had those other people involved."],[1775.69,"But they're not mentioned."],[1776.97,"So I mean, we don't know for sure."],[1778.49,"It's not impossible that he he wrote it himself."],[1783.45,"And that's also a possibility in terms of the two versions."],[1788.17,"That one the early version represents one that he wrote himself, and then the second version is sort of a more prepared for publication, you know, uh polished version that was sent out."],[1800.73,"Um, but so yeah, we don't have any direct direct indication the way we. do with a lot of uh St"],[1807.49,"Paul's writings that there was this other person who was acting as scribe, but this is their name."],[1813.57,"Um so uh what when it was written, uh generally uh St."],[1822.93,"Luke's Gospel and the Acts of the Apostles are dated to around 80 AD."],[1830.29,"Um the people who want to argue that Acts leaves off where it does because that's when it was written."],[1837.45,"Have to say it was written much, much earlier."],[1840.61,"Um there's there are some there are some issues with that in terms of uh structuring and in the sense that if if you're sort of recording things while they're happening, you know, there is a sort of this overarching structure."],[1861.77,"We take for granted when we read a biography of someone's life or a history book, you know."],[1868.41,"They're written sort of the same way you'd write a novel."],[1871.47,"There's a beginning and a middle and an end, and there are these themes and these kind of things, and events are put in perspective, and you choose certain things to include and certain things to leave out to communicate what you want to communicate."],[1882.27,"Well, real life is a lot more messy than that."],[1886.04,"There are a lot of days where Abraham Lincoln woke up, you know, ran his hands through his hair, went and did some paperwork at his desk, and went to bed."],[1894.52,"You know, that's a whole lot of days like that"],[1898.72,"Those are never in the biography."],[1900.34,"Those are never in the, you know, you don't always know at the time what events are going to end up being really significant."],[1906.18,"And you don't usually at the time have the perspective to say, you know, when I look back on this, this is gonna be, you know, and this other thing that seems important now, I'm gonna forget about in two weeks, you know."],[1920.02,"Um, and so the way that the the book of Acts is structured, you know, the way it treats the people who are in it and St."],[1928.58,"Paul and everything, it's clear that there's there's some hindsight going on, you know, there's some structuring in this story, you know, in terms of what's important, in terms of what themes and what St."],[1939.22,"And that implies that some time has passed"],[1939.74,"Luke wants to communicate."],[1943.9,"You know, that implies that there's been some time for discussion."],[1948.86,"Also, we have to remember that that uh you know, we take for granted that that uh the folks who wrote the New Testament documents just really wanted to sit down and write, you know, or thought they were writing Bible."],[1964.7,"You know, they said one day, hey, you know, we've got this old testament, and it's great, but we need a new testament, you know, so I'm gonna write part of it right now, you know."],[1974.3,"And none of them were thinking that."],[1977.28,"The the the all of the books of the New Testament were written because of particular situations in particular needs."],[1983.72,"Because when St"],[1984.32,"Now, this is obvious with the letters."],[1987.9,"Paul writes to the Corinthians, he says, I needed to write you this letter because this is going on and this is going on, this is and we need to fix this."],[1996.12,"Or to the Thessalonians or to whoever he wrote to."],[1998.92,"You know, there's very clear situations addressed."],[2002.12,"But the same is true of the Gospels, for example."],[2006.84,"Uh, the reason why all of a sudden, around starting with with St."],[2011.88,"Mark around 69 AD, all of a sudden the gospels start being written is because all those eyewitnesses are passing away."],[2023.05,"You're getting to the point where you're about 35 years out. you know, and so at that time in history, it was rare for people to live past their 60s"],[2033.33,"You know, if you hit 70, that was you know pretty good at that time in history."],[2038.05,"Well, if you consider that means anybody who was in their 30s at the time Jesus died, they were getting into their 60s."],[2045.89,"They're hitting 70 around the time St."],[2048.21,"Mark's Gospel was written."],[2049.89,"Well, as all of these eyewitnesses are starting to die out, it becomes very, very important to get in writing, you know, what they saw, what they heard Jesus say."],[2061.91,"To have this recorded and and set down on paper."],[2068.47,"We another thing that tends to fly past us because we read it so much is the way the New Testament, when it quotes the Old Testament, it'll say, as it is written."],[2079.81,"Or it is written, and then they quote."],[2081.57,"We say, Oh, that's just how you introduce a quote."],[2083.97,"Well, no, because other authors at the time quoted people, and they didn't say, It is written."],[2091.57,"You know, they didn't say it is written in Plato or it is written in Homer."],[2096.42,"Because in the Jewish context, there were certain books that were laid up at the temple that were kept in the temple."],[2105.14,"That's how that's sort of the mark of what was scripture."],[2108.92,"What was what we now call the Old Testament."],[2110.84,"These are the books that are kept in the temple."],[2113.48,"And we talked already about how. expensive it was to write books at this time"],[2118.96,"So something being written, and especially something being written and kept in the temple."],[2124.53,"It is written meant."],[2126.99,"This is of utmost importance."],[2130.03,"This is of utmost importance."],[2132.03,"And so when the Gospels are being written, and when uh Saint Peter, we'll see what we get into 2 Peter, refers to St."],[2141.39,"Paul's writings as scriptures."],[2144.59,"As things, things that are written."],[2146.87,"is this This means that these things are being written are of utmost importance."],[2151.71,"Putting something down on parts of it to be preserved, you know, until in their minds, until the end of the world."],[2158.19,"You know, this is important"],[2160.31,"This needs to, this needs to go down to uh to future generations."],[2166.79,"So because the book of the Acts of the Apostles is the second part, the second volume of St."],[2173.91,"Luke's two-volume work, it would have been occasioned by the same thing."],[2178.95,"It would have been occasioned by the same thing."],[2181.03,"Now, flip side of this is we have a lot of uh skeptics in the world today, guys like Bart Ehrman and the other guys you see interviewed in Horyist channel documentaries about the Bible, who want to dramatize how long it was af afterter the life and and death resurrection of Jesus that these things were written down."],[2207.71,"You know, it's 35 years after."],[2212.37,"This big long time."],[2213.65,"You can't remember what people said 35 years ago, you know."],[2218.69,"In fact, Bart Bart Ehrman likes to give the example."],[2221.65,"He says, uh, he asks people if they can remember what Barack Obama said in his second inaugural address."],[2229.17,"And I'm like, first of all, who even watched that?"],[2231.09,"But second of all, who even listened to that closely?"],[2235.73,"But, you know, we live in a very different culture."],[2240.54,"Uh, none of us in this room are disciples of Barack Obama."],[2244.86,"If we were, if we were close friends of his, if we were people at his inner circle. people who had served in his White House, we might very well remember a lot of the things he said and said to us and said in front of us"],[2255.4,"We might have a lot of stories about him."],[2259.04,"Even 35 years later."],[2261.52,"But also, you know, and we're getting a little late even for this analogy."],[2267.76,"I'm uh I'm gonna use."],[2269.68,"But uh if I wanted to today write a history of the Vietnam War, okay."],[2280.0,"That actually ended more than 35 years ago now."],[2281.92,"years ago now. It ended about 43"],[2285.6,"But we'll say roughly the same amount of time."],[2288.16,"I could find Vietnam veterans."],[2291.06,"I could find Vietnam veterans who could give me first hand accounts of what happened there."],[2296.62,"And if I interviewed enough of them, I could get a pretty good history together, a pretty accurate detailed history from first hand experience of the Vietnam War."],[2308.62,"What if I tried to do that with World War II right now?"],[2313.9,"When I was a kid, I could have done that with World War II."],[2317.76,"But today, if I tried to do that with World War II, it would be awfully spotty."],[2323.04,"We still have some World War II veterans with us, but it's a decreasing number."],[2326.88,"And I would only get bits and pieces."],[2329.76,"And if I tried to do World War I, it would be impossible"],[2334.2,"I couldn't do an oral history of World War I."],[2338.76,"So this shows us the basic difference between the books of the New Testament."],[2345.7,"Which are within that range."],[2348.2,"Where there were still people at the time they were written who could give them those firsthand accounts, where St."],[2353.16,"Luke could be telling the truth that he interviewed."],[2355.48,"You know, he talked to these people who had seen Jesus and heard Jesus themselves."],[2361.48,"And later Christian writings that are going to come in the second century, where we talk about you know the Gnostic Gospels, the Gospel of Thomas, and these these other other things."],[2373.24,"That conversely, the same people who say, Well, look, the you know, the Bible was written 35 years after."],[2378.8,"You know, they can't have known anything, but then something written in the second century, oh, this tells us all kinds of neat things about Jesus, you know, which makes no sense."],[2387.12,"But that shows you that difference."],[2388.65,"Because by the time things like the Gospel of Thomas or the Gospel of Mary Magdalene or any of these other things were being written, you know, that sort of they find one every Easter and every Christmas, so we can have more National Geographic Channel documentaries."],[2402.41,"By the time those were written, it was like World War I."],[2406.27,"Now, there was nobody."],[2408.51,"And you see that when you read them."],[2410.51,"They don't mention real places, you know, real geography, they don't mention real events, they don't because they don't have any connection anymore."],[2419.23,"But at the time the New Testament documents were written, that was still very possible."],[2423.87,"And one of the other things that St"],[2426.87,"Luke did in his gospel, and we'll see this again in the book of Acts, that shows us this is Saint Luke, as you're reading St."],[2433.75,"Luke's Gospel, he'll mention these seemingly random people, you know, who don't come up anywhere else in the story, and you're sort of like, okay, so like when he mentions that Simon of Cyrene carried the cross of Jesus, he said, His sons are Alexander and Rufus, and you're like, I care why."],[2454.13,"Well, the point is, Alexander and Rufus were still alive at the time Saint Luke wrote it."],[2461.55,"So, what St."],[2462.35,"Luke is is not so subtly saying is, you don't buy this? right"],[2469.35,"Or you need some proof, go talk to Alexander and Rufus, this man's sons."],[2474.23,"They'll tell you what happened."],[2477.53,"They'll they'll attest to this account."],[2482.17,"And when he talks about uh the Theotokos, he talks about Mary Christ's mother."],[2486.81,"Remember, he says, you know, Mary treasured up all these things in her heart."],[2490.49,"Well, what is he saying there?"],[2492.5,"He's saying, he's cluing you in, you know, this is where I got all this information about Jesus' conception."],[2500.58,"I talked to her and she remembered all these things because they happened to her and they were important."],[2505.54,"You know, they were very important to her."],[2507.94,"They concerned her and her son and you know, Joseph."],[2511.86,"So he very uh St."],[2512.1,"Luca particularly, again, not only uses that historical style, but really tethers"],[2519.74,"And we're gonna see the same thing in the book of Acts."],[2521.74,"We're gonna see these people who get just mentioned here and there, and you're like, why is that?"],[2528.38,"You know, okay, he stayed at so and so's house."],[2531.1,"Well, well, there were people who were reading this who knew so and so."],[2535.44,"And you can say, Yeah, I remember when St."],[2538.08,"Paul came and visited and you know stayed in my house."],[2542.08,"And that's right, those things did happen."],[2544.32,"You know, he did give that sermon, and those people were converted, and these other people did try to stone him, and you know, they could attest to attest to what happened."],[2554.32,"Okay. So, last note before we actually get started in the book, and that is that as we're going to see right from the beginning, again, as I mentioned, this is a planned two-volume work."],[2567.72,"And so uh St."],[2569.16,"Luke is really going to present what the apostles do as a continuation of what Jesus did."],[2578.12,"That Jesus doesn't ascend into heaven and then, well, it's all over."],[2581.72,"Go back to fishing."],[2584.33,"But that that is going to continue."],[2586.73,"And we're going to see this this uh transition, which is is not as rigid as people like to make out."],[2592.89,"You'll hear people say that that the eleven, not the 12, because Judas is gone, but uh the other that they wereed disciples before the resurrection and apostles afterward it's not quite that clean again the book is call the Acts of the Apostles and they are referred to the as the apostles that comes from uh the the Greek verb apostelo which means I I send it's the noun form so it's one who is sent it's somebody who you send uh so it's not just that these are these are Jesus disciples and so they sort of take over the movement now that Jesus is gone but they've been sent out by Jesus on a particular mission."],[2636.25,"And the Hebrew word that's usually translated uh with apostle in the Old Testament is Shaliach, which likewise means a person who is sent."],[2643.89,"in the But con in the Hebrew context, and in the Old Testament context, that word was used most often for the prophets, and it referred not just to someone who you send to do something, but someone who you send is a representative of yourself."],[2666.89,"So I send my representative, I send, or you send a diplomat."],[2671.13,"You know, a king sends a diplomat, someone to represent you."],[2675.93,"In later Hebrew usage, that word started being used for lawyers But it has that again a representative."],[2685.11,"This is the person who is going to represent me and my and my interests."],[2690.23,"So the apostle apostle isn't just a term of authority, like a title, you know, the apostle so and so."],[2698.07,"But the apostle is is their role and who they are and what they're doing."],[2702.55,"They've been sent out by Jesus to represent him in the world now that he is has ascended into heaven."],[2715.35,"Yeah, Saint Saint Luke, uh there are uh at least two still existing uh icons that were painted by Saint Luke."],[2724.95,"Um and being being uh having an artistic talent was part of being educated at that time."],[2738.03,"One is on Mount Athos."],[2740.43,"And I'm trying to remember where the second one is."],[2744.11,"I'll find out for next time."],[2746.27,"And they're both painted on wax tablets, which is what they did in the first century."],[2750.43,"We think of icons as being painted out of wood, but they didn't portraits on uh wax tablets in the first century."],[2758.35,"Um and uh yeah, we too we our education system is very science and math heavy."],[2765.23,"You know, we think, well, this is this is education, but education was much more broad-based in the ancient world."],[2771.55,"So when you when you were educated in the ancient world, you studied arithmetic, mathematics, and science, but you also and you studied philosophy and you studied literature and you studied art and music."],[2780.43,"You know, it was sort of the whole the whole spectrum"],[2786.79,"And so for someone to be educated enough to be a physician, having an artistic sort of sila was very common, whether that was painting or sculpture or some other or just art appreciation or something along those lines."],[2801.99,"So and having having known these people, as we mentioned, uh who he talked to who were eyewitnesses for his gospel, that would have given an opportunity to paint portraits as well."],[2813.59,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2820.95,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2823.11,"wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com. That's"]],"o":[{"t":21.68,"lv":2,"h":"Title: the Acts of the Apostles","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Starting <b>Acts</b> - an abbreviated title</li><li class=\"d0\">Real title: <b>The Acts of the Apostles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sometimes extended: <b>Acts of the Holy Apostles</b>, <b>Holy and All-Laudable Apostles</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Unique in the NT: most books are <b>Gospels</b> or <b>letters</b> (Paul, John, James, Peter)</li><li class=\"d1\">Revelation has letters attached - a bit closer</li></ul>"},{"t":57.12,"lv":2,"h":"A narrative of the church's beginnings","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Acts is a <b>story</b> - narrative about the beginnings of the church, the <b>apostles</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Picks up where <b>Luke</b> left off: <b>Christ's ascension</b></li><li class=\"d1\">story <b>repeated</b> as the second part begins</li><li class=\"d0\">Only narrative book in NT besides the Gospels</li><li class=\"d1\">Gospels center on <b>Jesus</b>; Acts centers on the <b>apostles</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Easy to miss how unique: whole Bible between <b>two covers</b></li><li class=\"d1\">OT has plenty of narratives about people who aren't Jesus</li></ul>"},{"t":164.2,"lv":2,"h":"OT stories are prophetic; Acts is after the fact","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">OT stories (<b>Moses</b>, <b>Abraham, Isaac, Jacob</b>, <b>David</b>, <b>Elijah, Elisha</b>) interpreted in NT as <b>pointing to Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Elijah</b> and <b>John the Baptist</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>David</b> and Jesus as Messiah</li><li class=\"d1\">we read them in a <b>prophetic</b> way</li><li class=\"d0\">Acts takes place <b>after Christ</b> - can't be read prophetically, \"after the fact\"</li></ul>"},{"t":225.28,"lv":3,"h":"The lives of the saints are significant","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Orthodox take this for granted; <b>Protestant friends</b> don't</li><li class=\"d1\">\"we just need Jesus\" - \"why ask a saint to pray for you? Just pray to Jesus\"</li><li class=\"d1\">true in a sense - Jesus is <b>God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">But Scripture shows <b>Peter, Paul, James</b>: their <b>acts</b>, what they did and taught, are <b>also significant</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Against <b>red-letter Bibles</b> - as if the words of Jesus are \"the parts that are really Bible\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"of course, that isn't the case\"</li></ul>"},{"t":313.65,"lv":3,"h":"Early church records the saints","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">From <b>near the very beginning</b>, church records stories of holy people</li><li class=\"d0\">Becomes the literature of the <b>lives of the saints</b></li><li class=\"d1\">2nd-3rd c. books called <b>Acts</b>: Acts of <b>Peter</b>, Acts of <b>Paul and Thecla</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Orthodox now: read the lives, <b>draw inspiration</b></li><li class=\"d0\">All called to be like Christ, but all <b>different</b>: men, women, young, old</li><li class=\"d1\">find saints <b>at whatever stage of life</b>, facing what we face</li><li class=\"d0\">Not accidental or a later development - <b>taught by Acts being in Scripture</b></li></ul>"},{"t":419.29,"lv":2,"h":"Author: St. Luke, a two-volume work","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Written by <b>St. Luke</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not just a second book - <b>second part of a two-part work</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Gospel of Luke + Acts</b> planned and put together</li><li class=\"d0\">Themes from Luke's Gospel carried forward</li><li class=\"d1\">\"seeds planted there\" now come to <b>fruition</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Connections between <b>life of Christ</b> and <b>lives of his apostles</b></li></ul>"},{"t":472.21,"lv":2,"h":"Theophilus the patron","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><i>Theophilus</i> = \"the man who loves God\"</li><li class=\"d1\">some say not a real person - Luke writing to <b>all who love God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">basis: wide scope, doesn't seem written for one person</li><li class=\"d0\">Answer: Theophilus most likely the <b>patron</b> who sponsored the writing</li><li class=\"d0\">Today: <b>self-publishing</b> on the internet</li><li class=\"d1\">then: <b>1.5 millennia before the printing press</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>vellum</b>, ink expensive; copies made <b>by hand</b>, one at a time</li><li class=\"d0\">Literacy very low - and in <b>Greek</b>, not just Aramaic</li></ul>"},{"t":603.0,"lv":3,"h":"Literacy then and now","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">WWI: US military standard for literate = <b>sign your name</b> rather than make your mark</li><li class=\"d1\">rate about <b>40%</b> - \"so they weren't reading books\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Average person reading = <b>very recent</b> (public education)</li><li class=\"d0\">Average person in 1st c. not literate</li><li class=\"d0\">But <b>more than one language</b> was common in the Roman world</li><li class=\"d1\">unlike America - other countries still common</li><li class=\"d1\">reading and writing is the rare thing</li></ul>"},{"t":723.26,"lv":3,"h":"The cost of a copy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">One NT book in 1st c. ~ <b>$5,000</b> today</li><li class=\"d0\">Luke's two volumes ~ <b>$10,000 per copy</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Wide distribution needs a <b>patron</b> to sponsor it</li><li class=\"d1\">Theophilus = the one <b>paying</b> to get it distributed</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>dedicated to</b> him rather than addressed to him per se</li></ul>"},{"t":789.8,"lv":2,"h":"Luke writes as a historian","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Hard to tell today: Bibles translated by <b>committee</b> - all sound the same</li><li class=\"d0\">In Greek, NT books have <b>vastly different styles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">can tell the <b>level of education</b> of each author</li><li class=\"d0\">Luke deliberately uses <b>Greek historians'</b> style - <b>Thucydides</b>, also <b>Herodotus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">conveys: \"this is a <b>historical account</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">compare <b>Hebrews</b>: more a sermon than a letter - different genres</li><li class=\"d0\">Educated readers would recognize it as <b>laying out history</b></li></ul>"},{"t":897.23,"lv":3,"h":"Not mythology","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Modern people want to turn NT into <b>mythology</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Greek mythological works exist - <b>Hesiod and Homer</b>, 800 BC</li><li class=\"d1\">Luke had that style available - <b>doesn't use it</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Chose historical style: \"things that <b>actually happened</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Gospel prologue: many written accounts, he compiled them, spoke to <b>eyewitnesses</b></li><li class=\"d1\">presents himself as a <b>historian who did his research</b></li></ul>"},{"t":965.39,"lv":3,"h":"The \"we\" sections","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">True for the first part of Acts</li><li class=\"d0\">Partway through (a little before halfway): switches from <b>third person</b> (\"he did, they did\") to <b>first person</b> (\"we did\")</li><li class=\"d1\">coincides with Luke <b>joining Paul</b> and companions on journeys</li><li class=\"d0\">Gospel: Luke <b>not an eyewitness</b>, spoke to witnesses</li><li class=\"d0\">Much of Acts = his <b>own eyewitness</b> testimony</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's letters mention Luke as a <b>companion</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1046.8,"lv":2,"h":"Luke the physician, from Antioch?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">From Paul's letters we learn Luke was a <b>physician</b></li><li class=\"d1\">first-century medicine very different - but based on study of the <b>human body</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not a \"witch doctor\" casting spells</li><li class=\"d0\">Meant he was in the <b>most educated class</b></li><li class=\"d1\">fluent in Greek, had read Greek histories and literature</li><li class=\"d0\">Generally argued (good case) he was from <b>Antioch</b></li><li class=\"d1\">large <b>Jewish population</b> from its founding - about <b>a third</b>, into 5th c.</li><li class=\"d1\">debated: Jewish, Greek, or between - <b>Assyrians</b>, Lebanese Semitic non-Jews</li><li class=\"d1\">\"we only have clues\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1208.9,"lv":2,"h":"Structure of Acts","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picks up at the <b>ascension</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Early Christian community, then transition to following <b>St. Paul</b></li><li class=\"d1\">after <b>Damascus road</b>, then <b>missionary journeys</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Just <b>leaves off</b> - doesn't end with Paul's death</li><li class=\"d1\">some say that's when it was written</li><li class=\"d1\">other good reasons to think written later - discuss at the end</li><li class=\"d0\">Gospel has clear unit: birth of <b>John the Forerunner</b> to <b>ascension</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Acts not really a life of Paul: <b>first dozen chapters</b> on a different group</li><li class=\"d1\">most drop out in second half to focus on Paul</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Interestingly structured book\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1311.42,"lv":2,"h":"Two versions of Acts","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Longer</b> and <b>shorter</b> version</li><li class=\"d1\">longer ~ <b>a third longer</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>no extra stories</b> - only description and detail</li><li class=\"d1\">e.g. the <b>shipwreck</b>: dramatic in long, bare bones in short</li><li class=\"d0\">Debated which came first</li><li class=\"d1\">shorter first and expanded? longer first and condensed for <b>copying cost</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">Church has used <b>both</b>, different times and places</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Archdiocese Epistle Book</b> (English) uses shorter, published ~5 years ago</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Slavonic</b> in Russia uses longer</li><li class=\"d0\">Oldest copies (back to early <b>2nd c.</b>) of both kinds</li></ul>"},{"t":1454.02,"lv":3,"h":"Both from Luke","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Theory he agrees with: <b>both came from Luke</b></li><li class=\"d1\">two different <b>drafts</b> - he wrote one and then edited it</li><li class=\"d0\">Copies of both circulated; churches copied <b>what they had</b></li><li class=\"d1\">a <b>mosaic</b> of who uses which</li><li class=\"d0\">Rome long, Alexandria short, Antioch long, Constantinople short - <b>no way to compare</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no phone calls: \"yours has a couple extra words\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Orthodox Study Bible</b> (<b>NKJV</b>) includes material from the longer version at times</li><li class=\"d1\">committee assessed case by case</li><li class=\"d1\">will note significant ones, not every word</li></ul>"},{"t":1599.43,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: did Luke dictate?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: apostles mostly dictated - did Luke write it himself?</li><li class=\"d0\">Hard to tell - he doesn't say</li><li class=\"d0\">Trick question: who wrote <b>Romans</b>? - <b>Tertius</b>, the scribe</li><li class=\"d1\">usually indicated at the end</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul sometimes: \"writing with my <b>own hand</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not because Paul couldn't write - <b>written and spoken word differ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like a secretary who polishes grammar</li><li class=\"d1\">procedure: dictate → written → author corrects → finished → delivered</li><li class=\"d0\">Letter <b>bearer</b> significant: no postal service</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>read it aloud</b>, prepared to <b>answer questions</b> about meaning</li><li class=\"d0\">Luke never says; possible he wrote it himself</li><li class=\"d1\">maybe early version = his own, second = <b>polished for publication</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1813.57,"lv":2,"h":"Date and why Acts was written","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Luke and Acts generally dated ~<b>80 AD</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Those who say Acts stops where it does because that's when written need a <b>much earlier</b> date</li><li class=\"d1\">issue: <b>structure</b> - biographies are shaped like novels: beginning, middle, end, themes</li><li class=\"d1\">real life is messier - Lincoln waking up, doing paperwork, going to bed</li><li class=\"d1\">you don't know at the time what will matter</li><li class=\"d0\">Acts shows <b>hindsight</b> and themes → <b>time has passed</b>, time for discussion</li></ul>"},{"t":1948.86,"lv":3,"h":"Why the NT was written when it was","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">NT authors weren't thinking \"I'm writing <b>Bible</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">All books written for <b>particular situations and needs</b></li><li class=\"d1\">obvious in letters: Corinthians, Thessalonians</li><li class=\"d0\">Same for Gospels: starting with <b>Mark ~69 AD</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>eyewitnesses passing away</b> - ~35 years out</li><li class=\"d1\">rare to live past <b>60s</b>; 70 was \"pretty good\"</li><li class=\"d1\">those in their 30s at the Cross now near 70</li><li class=\"d0\">Urgent to get in writing what they saw and heard</li></ul>"},{"t":2068.47,"lv":3,"h":"\"It is written\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">NT quoting OT: \"<b>it is written</b>\" - not just a way to introduce a quote</li><li class=\"d1\">others didn't say \"it is written in Plato or Homer\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Jewish context: books <b>kept in the temple</b> = mark of Scripture</li><li class=\"d0\">Writing was expensive → \"it is written\" = <b>of utmost importance</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2 Peter</b> calls Paul's writings <b>scriptures</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Preserved \"until the end of the world\" for <b>future generations</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Acts, as volume two, occasioned by <b>the same thing</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2181.03,"lv":2,"h":"Skeptics and the 35-year gap","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Skeptics: <b>Bart Ehrman</b>, History Channel documentary types</li><li class=\"d1\">dramatize: written <b>35 years</b> after - \"you can't remember\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Ehrman's example: Obama's <b>second inaugural address</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Response: we aren't <b>disciples</b> of Obama</li><li class=\"d1\">inner circle, White House staff → would remember sayings and stories even 35 years later</li></ul>"},{"t":2267.76,"lv":3,"h":"Vietnam, WWII, WWI analogy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">History of <b>Vietnam War</b> today (ended ~43 years ago): find veterans, get detailed <b>firsthand</b> history</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>WWII</b>: as a kid possible; now \"awfully spotty\", decreasing number</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>WWI</b>: impossible to do oral history</li><li class=\"d0\">NT documents fall <b>within the range</b> of living witnesses</li><li class=\"d1\">Luke can truly say he <b>interviewed</b> those who saw and heard Jesus</li><li class=\"d0\">2nd-c. writings (<b>Gospel of Thomas</b>, <b>Mary Magdalene</b>) - \"like WWI\", nobody left</li><li class=\"d1\">no real places, geography, or events - <b>no connection</b></li><li class=\"d1\">yet skeptics prize them: \"neat things about Jesus\" - \"makes no sense\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2423.87,"lv":2,"h":"Luke names living witnesses","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">In Gospel, mentions <b>seemingly random people</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Simon of Cyrene</b> carried the cross - sons <b>Alexander and Rufus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"I care why?\" - they were <b>still alive</b> when Luke wrote</li><li class=\"d1\">\"don't buy this? go talk to Alexander and Rufus\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Theotokos</b>: \"Mary <b>treasured up</b> all these things in her heart\"</li><li class=\"d1\">cluing you in: source for Jesus' <b>conception</b> - \"I talked to her\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same in Acts: people mentioned here and there</li><li class=\"d1\">\"he stayed at so and so's house\" - readers knew them: \"I remember when Paul stayed in my house\"</li><li class=\"d1\">sermon given, converts made, attempts to <b>stone</b> him - they could attest</li></ul>"},{"t":2554.32,"lv":2,"h":"Apostles continue what Jesus did","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Planned two-volume work: apostles' work = <b>continuation</b> of Jesus'</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"it's all over, go back to fishing\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Transition not as rigid as made out</li><li class=\"d1\">\"disciples before the resurrection, apostles afterward\" - not that clean</li><li class=\"d0\"><i>apostolos</i> from <i>apostello</i> = \"I send\" → one who is <b>sent</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just disciples taking over the movement</li><li class=\"d1\">sent by Jesus on a <b>particular mission</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2636.25,"lv":3,"h":"Shaliach: a representative","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew OT word: <i>shaliach</i> = person sent</li><li class=\"d0\">Usually used of the <b>prophets</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just sent to do something - sent as <b>representative of yourself</b></li><li class=\"d1\">king sends a <b>diplomat</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Later Hebrew: used for <b>lawyers</b> - one who represents me and my interests</li><li class=\"d0\">Apostle = <b>role</b>, not just title of authority</li><li class=\"d1\">sent to <b>represent him</b> in the world now that he has ascended</li></ul>"},{"t":2715.35,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: icons painted by Luke","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">At least <b>two surviving icons</b> painted by Luke</li><li class=\"d1\">one on <b>Mount Athos</b>; second - \"I'll find out for next time\"</li><li class=\"d1\">painted on <b>wax tablets</b>, 1st c. - not wood</li><li class=\"d0\">Art was part of ancient <b>education</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ours is <b>science and math heavy</b>; ancient: arithmetic, science, philosophy, literature, art, music</li><li class=\"d0\">Physician → artistic skill common: painting, sculpture, appreciation</li><li class=\"d0\">Knowing eyewitnesses gave opportunity to paint <b>portraits</b> too</li></ul>"}]});
